% TileStories — Thesis Preparation Report — Bibliography
% Fully reconstructed, reference by reference, with explicit per-entry
% platform/credibility scoring. This version supersedes all previous
% bibliography files for this report.
%
% HOW TO READ EACH ENTRY:
%   Every reference is preceded by one or more comment lines (%), never
%   compiled or printed, that record:
%     [BEST PLATFORM]    — the most credible platform/publisher hosting
%                           this work, with its tier (Tier 1 / Tier 2 /
%                           Tier N/A / Non-academic) per the venue list
%                           you provided.
%     [ACADEMIC SCORE]   — a 0-10 score with a short justification, using
%                           the rubric below.
%     [VERIFICATION NOTES] — present only where something needs tracking:
%                           an unconfirmed statistic from an earlier draft,
%                           a corrected misattribution, a cross-source
%                           discrepancy, or an action item.
%   These comment lines are where ALL the verification/process bookkeeping
%   now lives. The reference body itself (the actual @entry) carries only
%   standard bibliographic fields, plus a `note` field used ONLY where it
%   is genuinely necessary to justify a non-ideal source choice to a reader
%   of the final compiled bibliography (per your explicit instruction) —
%   most entries below have no `note` field at all for exactly this reason.
%
% SCORING RUBRIC (applied consistently across all 61 entries):
%   9-10  Tier-1 venue, very current (2023-2026), strong empirical rigour,
%         clean/large sample.
%   7-8   Tier-1 or strong Tier-2, current or foundational-and-unsuperseded,
%         solid methodology.
%   5-6   Real peer-reviewed venue but dated for a fast-moving field, OR a
%         foundational book/monograph (respected but not journal-reviewed).
%   3-4   Thesis/dissertation, ISO standard, practitioner classic, or an
%         official institutional/government source used for a factual claim.
%   1-2   Vendor documentation, museum/press pages, single-source informal
%         precedents — legitimate for the narrow factual claim they
%         support, but not academic literature.
%   0     Reserved for unbacked assertions; nothing in this bibliography
%         scores 0, because anything that would have, was independently
%         re-verified and either fixed or removed during this pass.
%   IMPORTANT: a low score is not a verdict that a source is "bad" — a
%   museum's own page describing its own opening hours is a perfectly
%   correct, appropriate citation for that specific claim, and is scored
%   low only because it is not academic literature, not because it is
%   misused.
%
% ON URLS: the most direct, credible link to the article itself is given
% wherever I could construct or confirm one with confidence (e.g. ACM's
% dl.acm.org/doi/ pattern, MDPI's own article URLs, PLOS, Frontiers, and
% Nature Portfolio's confirmed URL patterns). Where I was not fully
% confident in a hand-constructed publisher-specific URL (chiefly some
% Elsevier/Wiley/Oxford articles), the DOI resolver link (doi.org/...) is
% given instead, since it always resolves correctly to the publisher's own
% page even though it is one redirect away from the final URL.
%
% ON PAGES: for journal articles, the standard volume/number/pages locator
% is retained alongside the URL/DOI as normal complete bibliographic
% practice. For books, `pages` identifies the specific chapter or section
% the cited claim is drawn from, not a whole-book span (books don't have
% one) — e.g. vergo1989's pages={1--5} is Vergo's own introductory essay,
% not "the whole book."
%
% TWO REAL FIXES MADE DURING THIS PASS: `streetmuseum` and `iso9241210`
% were named in the chapter prose but never actually wrapped in a \cite{}
% command — both fixed directly in chapter-relatedwork.tex as part of this
% pass, so neither is an orphaned reference anymore.
